course: dictionary
lesson: 8407
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and «rump» is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning the writer means. «Roe deer are small, about 70 centimetres at the shoulder, with a white rump.» Deer have bodies and legs, so here rump means the back end of an animal's body, above the back legs.
explain: The same word can change its meaning when the topic changes. «Rump steak» is food, so the clue is steak, and rump means a cut of meat from that part of an animal. Read the word beside it to find the topic, then try the meaning in the sentence to check.
explain: Rump can also be a joke word for a person's bottom, or a small leftover part of a bigger group. In history, the «Rump Parliament» was what remained of the English Parliament after many members were thrown out. Clues like body, meat or history tell you which meaning to pick.
fact: Rump means the back end of an animal's body, above the back legs.
fact: «Rump steak» is a cut of meat from that part of an animal.
fact: Informal, jokey use: rump can mean a person's bottom, so keep it for chatty writing, not formal writing.
fact: A rump can also be a small leftover part of something bigger, such as the «Rump Parliament» (1648–1653), what was left of the English Parliament after many members were thrown out.
fact: When a word has several meanings, check the topic around it, then swap in your meaning to see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Roe deer are small, with a white rump.» What is a rump here? | The front legs of a deer | A small leftover group after most have left | A soft cut of meat from a farm | *The back end of an animal's body, above the back legs
exercise: Dad orders «rump steak» at the café. What is the rump here? | *A cut of meat from the back end of an animal | A small group of people | The top of a hill | A piece of bread
exercise: In history, the «Rump Parliament» was what was left of the English Parliament after many members were thrown out. What does rump mean in this sense? | A brand new, bigger group of members | *A small leftover part of something bigger | A loud shout in the House | A bird's tail feathers
test: After most members left, only a rump of the group was still talking. What does rump mean? | A grand new building | A long piece of string | *A small leftover part of a bigger group | A loud piece of music
test: Tom laughed and said he had fallen on his rump in the snow. What does rump mean here? | *A person's bottom, said as a joke | A small leftover part | The back legs of a horse | A cut of meat
exam: Which sentence uses rump as a leftover part of a bigger group, not as a body part or food? | «The horse swished its rump to shoo away a fly.» | «The roe deer ran off, its white rump flashing in the trees.» | *«The Rump Parliament met in 1648.» | «She ordered rump steak with chips.»
